# Full-depth felt-experience overrides (loaded last). Batch: crack, mephedrone, PCP, DXM, ADHD stimulants.
from gen import D
F={}
F["crack-cocaine"]={
"onset":["Crack is cocaine in a smokable base form, and smoking delivers it to the brain within seconds. The body reacts almost instantly: the heart pounds hard and fast, the chest tightens, skin flushes or goes clammy, pupils widen and a ringing or rushing sound may fill the ears.","Mentally the onset is an explosive rush of intense euphoria, confidence and power, often described as the most intense pleasure a person has ever felt, compressed into a minute or two. Every thought feels urgent and brilliant, and the craving to repeat it starts almost immediately.","That speed is what makes crack so much more compulsive than snorted powder. Chest pain, a sense of the heart racing out of control or sudden severe headache in this window can signal heart attack or stroke and need emergency help."],
"peak":["The peak is brief, usually only several minutes. The body is wired and tense: jaw clenched, muscles twitchy, breathing quick, heart rate and blood pressure high, appetite gone and the mouth bone-dry.","Mentally the high is sharp, electric and self-assured, but it already carries an edge of watchfulness. Many people grow hyper-alert to sounds and movement, check windows and doors, or feel that others are watching them, an early taste of stimulant paranoia.","Because the peak is so short, people typically use again and again in binges. Each round raises heart strain and body temperature, and mixing with alcohol forms cocaethylene, which is more toxic to the heart."],
"plateau":["There is barely a true plateau. Within ten to twenty minutes the body is left jittery and depleted: sweating, restless legs, shaky hands, a racing pulse that does not match the fading pleasure, and sometimes a burning throat or cough from the smoke.","Mentally what remains is a buzzing anxiety and a fierce, consuming craving for more. Thinking narrows to getting the next hit; time, money, food, sleep and other people fall out of view. With continued use, paranoia, hearing things and suspicious searching ('ghost-chasing') can take over.","Binges can last hours or days. Overheating, seizures, irregular heartbeat and lung injury ('crack lung') are the main dangers, and street supplies may contain fentanyl, so naloxone and not using alone matter."],
"comedown":["The crash is fast and heavy. The body feels drained, achy and shaky, the heart may still flutter, and extreme exhaustion sets in, often followed by long sleep and ravenous hunger once the drug clears.","Emotionally the comedown is notoriously bleak: a hollow, irritable, anxious despair, sometimes with guilt and hopelessness or thoughts of suicide. Pleasure in ordinary things can feel absent for days.","Repeated use causes strong psychological dependence with intense cravings triggered by cues. There is no approved medicine for cocaine dependence yet, but counseling approaches such as contingency management work, and help lines can connect people to treatment."]}
F["mephedrone"]={
"onset":["Mephedrone (4-MMC, 'meow meow') is a synthetic cathinone that was legally sold in the UK until 2010. Swallowed it builds over a short while; snorted it hits within minutes. The body warms, the heart speeds up, pupils widen and a tingling rush runs up the spine and scalp, often with jaw tension and teeth grinding.","Mentally the onset is a surging euphoria that people compare to a blend of MDMA and cocaine: an upwelling of affection, talkativeness and confidence, music sounding richer and touch feeling good.","The rush fades fast, and the urge to redose arrives early and strongly, which is the defining trap of mephedrone. Cold or bluish fingers and toes can appear because it tightens blood vessels."],
"peak":["At its peak the body feels energetic and hot, with sweating, a pounding pulse, clenched jaw, restlessness and loss of appetite. Some notice a distinctive body odor and blotchy skin.","Mentally the peak is warm and sociable: people feel open, close to others and deeply interested in conversation, with a stimulating clarity. It is shallower and shorter than MDMA, and there is often a background nervousness.","Overheating, a racing or irregular heartbeat, agitation and seizures are the main dangers, and they rise with redosing and with alcohol or other stimulants."],
"plateau":["The plateau is short and choppy, usually an hour or less per dose, so a session becomes a cycle of redosing. The body stays wired, dehydrated and tense, with tightened blood vessels, nosebleeds if snorted and difficulty urinating.","Mentally each wave brings less euphoria and more anxiety, restlessness and fixation on taking more. Some people become paranoid, agitated or confused, especially after a long night.","Large or repeated amounts have caused deaths from overheating and heart problems; anyone hot, confused or with chest pain needs emergency care."],
"comedown":["The comedown brings exhaustion, aching muscles, a sore jaw, poor sleep and sometimes chest tightness or a racing heart that keeps people awake.","Emotionally it is often harsh: low mood, anxiety, irritability and intense craving, sometimes for days. Memory and concentration feel fuzzy.","Frequent use produces strong compulsive use and dependence; support services and talking therapies can help, and people should avoid mixing it with other stimulants or alcohol."]}
F["pcp"]={
"onset":["PCP (phencyclidine, 'angel dust') was developed as an anesthetic in the 1950s and dropped because patients woke agitated and delirious. Smoked, it comes on within minutes; swallowed, over a longer while. The body feels numb and distant, especially in the hands and feet, movements become stiff and clumsy, and eyes may flick rapidly side to side (nystagmus).","Mentally the onset is a growing sense of detachment: the body feels rubbery or not one's own, sounds become echoey and distant, and thoughts begin to fragment. Some feel euphoric or powerful; others feel confused and threatened.","PCP's effect is unpredictable even for experienced users, and its numbing means people can be badly hurt without feeling it."],
"peak":["At its peak the body may be rigid or floppy, with high blood pressure, a fast heart, sweating, drooling and a blank stare. Pain is blocked, and people can show startling strength because they do not register strain.","Mentally the peak ranges from dreamy dissociation to frank psychosis: distorted body image, hallucinations, paranoia, grandiosity and loss of contact with reality. Some become withdrawn and catatonic; others become agitated, terrified or violent.","Dangers include severe injuries, drowning, seizures, very high body temperature and blood pressure, muscle breakdown that harms the kidneys, and coma. Emergency care is needed for agitation, overheating or unresponsiveness."],
"plateau":["The plateau is long and uneven, lasting hours, with waves of clarity and confusion. The body stays numb and uncoordinated, blood pressure high, and speech slurred or halting.","Mentally people may drift between dreamlike calm and sudden fear or anger, often without remembering what triggered it. Time, place and identity can feel scrambled.","Because PCP is stored in body fat, effects can resurface or linger for a day or more, and staying in a quiet, low-stimulation place with sober help reduces danger."],
"comedown":["The comedown is slow and blurry: fatigue, muscle soreness, headache and poor coordination may last into the next day.","Emotionally people often feel confused, anxious, depressed or ashamed, with patchy memory; some experience lingering paranoia or psychosis for days.","Regular use can cause memory problems, speech difficulties, depression and lasting psychotic symptoms; mental-health care is important if symptoms persist."]}
F["dxm"]={
"plateau":["DXM's plateau is long, several hours, and its character depends heavily on amount. The body often feels heavy, robotic and uncoordinated, with a stiff, mechanical walk ('robo-walk'), itching, sweating, a fast heart and nausea that comes and goes. Many products also contain other drugs, such as acetaminophen or antihistamines, which add their own dangers.","Mentally the plateau ranges from a mild, music-enhancing dreaminess at lower amounts to deep dissociation at higher amounts: a sense of being separated from the body, slowed or looping thoughts, closed-eye dreamscapes and losing track of where one is. Some feel content and reflective; others feel confused or trapped.","Danger rises with serotonin-affecting antidepressants (serotonin syndrome), alcohol, and products containing acetaminophen, which can silently destroy the liver. Overheating, seizures and vomiting while sedated are emergencies."],
"comedown":["The comedown is slow and often unpleasant: a groggy, flat, hungover feeling with headache, fatigue, dizziness and lingering clumsiness, sometimes into the next day. Sleep can be poor.","Emotionally people may feel detached, low, anxious or mentally slowed for a day or more, with a sense that the world is slightly unreal ('afterglow' for some, gloom for others).","Frequent use can lead to dependence, memory problems and depression. Anyone who used a combination cold product should get medical advice about acetaminophen exposure even if they feel fine."]}
F["lisdexamfetamine"]={
"onset":["Lisdexamfetamine (Vyvanse, Elvanse) is a prodrug: it is inactive until enzymes in the blood slowly cut it into dextroamphetamine. Swallowed, it comes on gradually, with no sharp rush. Over the first hour or two the body warms slightly, the heart picks up a little, the mouth dries and appetite quietly fades.","Mentally the onset feels like a fog lifting: background restlessness settles for people with ADHD, tasks look more manageable and the mind locks onto what is in front of it. People without ADHD may feel a smooth lift in mood, energy and talkativeness.","Its slow conversion was designed to make other routes no faster and to reduce misuse, though it is still an amphetamine with the same risks at high amounts."],
"peak":["At its peak the body is alert and slightly tense: faster pulse, raised blood pressure, dry mouth, clenched jaw for some, and little interest in food. Sweating and a mild tremor can appear.","Mentally the peak is steady focus, motivation and confidence. Tasks feel rewarding, and people may work for hours without noticing time. At higher amounts this becomes edgy, overly talkative and anxious, with racing thoughts.","Risks include high blood pressure, heart rhythm problems, anxiety, and, rarely, psychosis or mania, especially with high amounts or sleep loss."],
"plateau":["The plateau is long, often most of the day, which is part of why it is popular for ADHD. Physically people stay mildly wired, with low appetite, dry mouth and sometimes headaches or a tight jaw.","Mentally attention stays steady, though some feel flat, overly serious or emotionally muted ('zombie' feeling), and irritability can build late in the day.","Taking it late or in extra amounts causes insomnia, and skipping meals adds to crash and mood problems."],
"comedown":["As it wears off, often in the evening, people feel tired, hungry, headachy and sometimes achy.","Emotionally a 'rebound' of irritability, low mood, tearfulness or returning ADHD restlessness is common, usually milder than with short-acting amphetamine.","Misuse or high amounts can lead to dependence and a heavier crash with depression and sleepiness; any change in use should be discussed with the prescriber."]}
F["methylphenidate"]={
"onset":["Methylphenidate (Ritalin, Concerta, Medikinet) blocks the reuptake of dopamine and noradrenaline. Immediate-release tablets come on within a short while; extended-release forms like Concerta rise in stages over the day. The body perks up gently: a slightly faster heart, a dry mouth, a sense of tension in the shoulders or jaw and fading hunger.","Mentally the onset is a sharpening: the noise of distraction quiets, the urge to fidget fades in people with ADHD, and focus narrows onto the task. People without ADHD may feel a clean, mildly euphoric alertness, often described as 'cleaner' or 'drier' than amphetamine.","Stomachaches and headaches are common early, especially in children."],
"peak":["At its peak the body feels alert and wired, with raised pulse and blood pressure, sweating, and sometimes a tremor or tense muscles.","Mentally there is focus and drive; at higher amounts it becomes jittery, anxious, irritable and obsessive, with racing thoughts and less euphoria than amphetamine.","Risks include heart rhythm problems, high blood pressure and, rarely, psychosis. Misusing crushed tablets in other ways causes serious harm because of the fillers."],
"plateau":["Immediate-release tablets give a plateau of a few hours; extended-release keeps a steady level for much of the day. The body stays mildly tense, with low appetite and dry mouth.","Mentally attention holds steady, though some feel emotionally flat, rigid or irritable.","Doses late in the day disrupt sleep."],
"comedown":["As it fades, people may feel tired, hungry and headachy, sometimes with a sudden drop in energy.","Emotionally a rebound of irritability, tearfulness or restlessness is common, especially in children in the late afternoon.","Misuse at high amounts can lead to dependence and a crash with low mood and exhaustion."]}
for k,v in F.items():
    if k in D: D[k].setdefault("feel",{}).update(v)
